Syntax: keycut <direction>

A looter who cannot pick a lock may still copy it.  Keycut takes a wax
impression of a door's lock and casts a working duplicate of its key.

The door must have a real keyhole.  Doors barred, stuck or held shut by
something other than a lock offer nothing to take an impression from.

Casting consumes wax from your inventory.  The amount needed scales with the
size of the key being copied, and an unpracticed hand spoils a good deal more
of it than a master does.  The wax is spent whether or not the casting takes,
so a failed attempt is a real loss.

A wax key works exactly as the true key does, and will open every lock the
original opens.  It is also plainly a wax casting to anyone who looks at it,
and it will not last.  Within a few minutes it softens and falls apart in your
hands, so cast it where you mean to use it.

Taking an impression demands a steadiness of hand that is not quickly
recovered.  Once you have made the attempt you will be unable to make another
for about an hour.  This holds whether the casting succeeded or failed, so do
not begin one you are not prepared to see through.

See Also: PICKLOCK, JAM
